ed to retrieve up to 20 results from their respective datasets.The user is asking me to act as an Agent Blueprint expert for `earthscope_station_catalog`. I've been provided with:\n\n1. A system prompt with detailed rules about how to filter and rank EarthScope GNSS stations\n2. A question asking me to filter the staged EarthScope station metadata to stations within 50 km of Los Angeles center (34.0536909\u00b0N, -118.242766\u00b0W)\n3. A trajectory showing my previous work:\n   - First, I used shell_bash to peek at the CSV structure\n   - Then, I identified the columns: Site (station ID), Latitude, and (deg) (longitude, despite its odd header name)\n   - I called geo_filter_points_by_radius with the correct parameters\n   - The tool returned successfully with `within_radius_count: 72` stations within the 50 km radius, sorted by distance\n\nNow I need to:\n1. Analyze the results from the geo_filter_points_by_radius call\n2. Build the output JSON with `workflow_state` containing:\n   - `station_catalog` with status \"ranked\", candidate_count, and station_ids list\n   - `resource_discovery` with status and station_resource_queries\n\nLet me process the results:\n\nFrom observation_1, I have:\n- `within_radius_count: 72` \u2713 This is > 0, so we have coverage\n- `skipped_invalid: 0` \u2713 Perfect parsing with no errors\n- `points`: Array of 72 stations, each with Site, Latitude, (deg), distance_km, and id\n\nThe stations are already sorted by distance_km (ascending, nearest first). I need to extract the station IDs in order and create resource queries.\n\nStation IDs in order (from the points array):\n1. MTA1 (0.3045 km)\n2. PKRD (2.1848 km)\n3. ELSC (4.1351 km)\n4. USC2 (4.9583 km)\n5. SILK (5.8032 km)\n6. OXYC (8.9684 km)\n7. P800 (10.4218 km)\n8. LEE2 (11.5697 km)\n9. LEEP (11.5697 km)\n10. GVRS (11.9847 km)\n... and 62 more\n\nThe system prompt indicates I should set `station_catalog.status = \"ranked\"`, include all 72 stations in `station_ids` (since they're all valid within-radius results), and create a resource query for each one with an `ndp_search_datasets` call. The resolver will receive the full ranked list and can decide which stations to actually stage for data retrieval. I need to complete the partial thought from the current rewriting. The previous rewriting ends mid-entry at `{\"station\": \"WMAP\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"WMAP\", \"limit\": 20}}]},` and then cuts off at `{\"station\":`. \n\nLet me complete this by copying from the next thinking up to a logical break point:\n\n\"TORP\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"TORP\", \"limit\": 20}}]},\n        {\"station\": \"LBCH\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"LBCH\", \"limit\": 20}}]},\n        {\"station\": \"LBC2\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"LBC2\", \"limit\": 20}}]},\n        {\"station\": \"HBCO\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"HBCO\", \"limit\": 20}}]},\n        {\"station\": \"WCHS\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"WCHS\", \"limit\": 20}}]},\n        {\"station\": \"CVHS\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"CVHS\", \"limit\": 20}}]},\n        {\"station\": \"PVRS\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"PVRS\", \"limit\": 20}}]},\n        {\"station\": \"SNHS\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"SNHS\", \"limit\": 20}}]},\n        {\"station\": \"PVHS\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"PVHS\", \"limit\": 20}}]},\n        {\"station\": \"AZU1\", \"preferred_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"ndp_search_datasets\", \"arguments\": {\"dataset_title\": \"AZU1\", \"limit\": 20}}]},\n        {\"station\": \"CGD\n\nThe next thinking is still incomplete, cutting off at \"CGD\" without finishing the entry.\n\nI notice the instructions specify returning \"up to several candidates (three or more when available)\" but I'm currently including many more stations in the list. I should reconsider whether this full list aligns with that guidance, or if I need to limit to a smaller subset of the most relevant nearby stations. the resolver with nothing to do. I need to construct `resource_discovery.station_resource_queries` by iterating through the ranked `points` and creating a `preferred_calls` entry for each station I decide to include.\n\nThe guidance about \"several candidates (three or more when available)\" suggests I shouldn't include all 72 stations\u2014that would overwhelm the resolver. Instead, I should select a manageable subset of the nearest ones. Given the context, returning the top 10-15 closest stations seems reasonable: it provides enough options for the resolver to work with while staying practical.\n\nBut looking at the example output showing `candidate_count: 9`, that gives me a concrete reference point. The key instruction is to return candidates ranked by distance and network diversity so the resolver has fallbacks. I think the phrase \"up to several\" actually means I should return all the within-radius stations, but the resolver will use them in priority order based on the ranking. the resolver. The resolver is responsible for selection and staging, not me. I should return all 72 stations ranked by distance and let the resolver decide which ones to keep based on its own criteria. The warnings about empty station_ids and the emphasis on providing fallbacks reinforce that more options serve the resolver better than fewer. The critical constraint is clear: leaving `station_ids` empty would leave the 